          "How it reached England is worth setting out exactly. Cyril Lucar, patriarch first of Alexandria and then of Constantinople, brought it from Egypt to Constantinople in 1621. He offered it in 1625 through Sir Thomas Roe, the English ambassador, as a New Year's gift to King James — who died before the gift took effect, so that, in Kenyon's words, it \"was actually received by Charles I. in 1627\". Nor did it enter the British Museum in 1753, the year the Museum was founded: it came with the Old Royal Library, which George II presented to the nation in 1757. Earlier still, an Arabic note in the first volume records the codex bound to the patriarchal cell in Alexandria, with a curse on whoever removed it.",
